FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1342 — `declared_module_functions.insert(name.clone(), published)` keys the published-function table by module **name**, while `module_functions` and `module_declared` are keyed by module **binding**. A second module with the same name (a redeclaration, or a module in another block) overwrites the first module's table at close time; the deferred pass at src/lsp.rs:2170-2176 then looks the table up by name and can resolve a call bound to the earlier module against the later module's function set, or answer `None` when the earlier module did publish the member. The runtime keeps distinct module values, so a call that executes can fail to navigate. — Fix: key the table by the module binding id (the deferred pass already holds `receiver_binding`) and look it up by that binding, matching the binding-keyed `module_functions`. A fixture with two same-named modules, a call bound to the first, and a later declaration of the second would settle it; I could not run one within the review budget, so this is not marked BLOCKER.

2. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1631 — `module_functions` is only ever inserted on a `to` declaration (grep shows the map is touched at 1255, 1336 and 1631 only). If a module top-level `set`/`constant` later rebinds the same name (the `module_declared` path records such names, src/lsp.rs:1771), the stale `to` binding remains in `module_functions`, so `published` still maps the name to the shadowed function. `M.f()` then navigates to a declaration that no longer holds the module's member value. — Fix: on a module-top-level `set`/`constant`, remove or replace the same-name entry in `module_functions`. No test covers `to f` followed by `set f` inside a module.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:2162 — The new declared-module branch is gated on `call_follows(&tokens, index)`, so only `M.name()` resolves. A bare module-member read `M.name` (a function value, which the interpreter exposes) falls through to the object-member branch, where the module binding has no field/method table, and `goto_definition`/`find_references` therefore answer `None`/empty even though completion at src/lsp.rs:2547 does offer the member. — Fix: resolve a non-call member against `declared_module_functions` as well, or pin the deliberate exclusion with a test. The four new tests only exercise calls, so this gap is untested.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
